UPDATE term_articles SET intro='Al-Bīrūnī (§261) identifies a solar eclipse with the Moon interposing between an observer and the solar disk. He then explains that its apparent magnitude differs by place owing to lunar parallax (§262). Unlike a lunar eclipse, the local appearance is not shared uniformly across the sunlit part of Earth.',editorial_notes='Batch 06: redundant definition lead suppressed or replaced with primary-source distinction.' WHERE term_id=(SELECT id FROM terms WHERE slug='solar-eclipse');
UPDATE term_articles SET intro='Al-Bīrūnī (§255) describes the full Moon entering Earth''s shadow at opposition when its latitude is sufficiently small. The shadow affects the Moon itself, although whether observers can see the eclipse depends on their local night (§260). This differs from the local parallax governing solar-eclipse visibility.',editorial_notes='Batch 06: redundant definition lead suppressed or replaced with primary-source distinction.' WHERE term_id=(SELECT id FROM terms WHERE slug='lunar-eclipse');
